  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Gel dan Kabut (the gel and the haze)",
    "overview": "Every night of the week has its own light. A page is a hazy room washed by the gel of the night on stage: strongest at the rig at the top, falling away into blackout. Only one gel is lit at a time. Changing nights is a lighting cue, not a slide change: the text leaves, the crystals pan out of the room, the gel cross-fades, the next night's crystals pan in, and its name arrives letter by letter.\n\nThe controls are not part of the show. Buttons, fields and the ticket are lit by the work light (tungsten), never by the gel, so the colour on stage always means \"which night\" and never \"click here\". Luxury comes from restraint: thin tall type, sharp 2 px corners, near-black ground, and motion that has a cause. There are no photos yet; photo frames carry a labelled brief until the owner's photos arrive.\n\nThe same world runs through three regions. The public site (persuade) carries the full room. The guest pass (offline) carries a band of the night's room above a light ticket. The door scanner and the manager panel (operate) keep the gel as a thin accent and a single washed band; at the door, speed and contrast beat the look.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One gel per weekday (Thursday blue, Friday amber, Saturday red, Sunday rose), one on stage at a time",
      "Flat geometric crystal shards in three layers, one composition per night, idle drift and a pan cue between nights",
      "Big Shoulders Display at weight 100 for night names, Albert Sans for everything read",
      "Tungsten primary buttons; the gel never fills a control",
      "2 px corners everywhere; the only round shapes are radio dots and status lights"
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Gel Rule",
        "body": "Only one night's gel is lit on a screen. Other nights appear only as their swatch's thin top edge.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Work Light Rule",
        "body": "Controls are tungsten or outline. A button filled with gel colour is a bug.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Thin Beam Rule",
        "body": "Display type is never heavier than 300 except the mark. No italics anywhere; `em` and `i` render upright.",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** take the night's colours from its gel class (`gel-thu` to `gel-sun`) through `gelOf(date)`; never hard-code a gel hex in a component.",
      "**Do** keep primary buttons tungsten and secondary buttons outlined.",
      "**Do** use 2 px corners on every new box, badge or count.",
      "**Do** set night names in Big Shoulders Display 100, uppercase, and everything read in Albert Sans.",
      "**Do** give text over the lit room the dark halo (`room-in`) and dim crystals behind flow titles.",
      "**Do** honour `prefers-reduced-motion` in every new animation; fades only.",
      "**Do** keep photo frames as labelled briefs until the owner adds real photos."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** fill a button, field or control with gel colour.",
      "**Don't** light two gels at once on one screen.",
      "**Don't** use drop shadows, glass blur, bevels or glow on panels; depth is light, not elevation.",
      "**Don't** use pill shapes or round corners above 2 px.",
      "**Don't** use italics, em dashes in interface text, or display type heavier than 300 outside the mark.",
      "**Don't** invent photos, testimonials, press or visitor numbers."
    ]
  }
}